<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<sideBySide>
<stack>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Complete the sentence:</p>

<p>Let <m>f(x) = \ln x</m>. Then, <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{2}</m> implies that ... 
  <answer name="v1Review1a">
    <choice><m>x = 2</m></choice>
    <choice><m>\bigg(\frac{1}{2}\bigg)^x = e</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1"><m>x = e^{\frac{1}{2}}</m></choice>
  </answer>
</p>

  <feedback condition="$_choice1"><p>Oops - You're thinking too far ahead and applying the rule for taking the <em>derivative</em> of the <term>natural logarithm function</term>. While it's true that <m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{2}</m> would imply that <m>x = 2</m>, what's being asked here is what <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{2}</m> implies.  This is a question about the basic definition of the natural logarithm function, which you can read about <ref to="https://mathinsight.org/logarithm_basics">here</ref>. 
</p></feedback>

  <feedback condition="$_choice2"><p>This is incorrect. 
The <term>natural logarithm function</term> takes as an input a number <m>x</m>, and returns as its output the value which the number <m>e</m> must be "raised to" which results in that particular <m>x</m> value. So, <m>\ln(x) = \frac{1}{2}</m> means that you must raise the number <m>e</m> to the power <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> for that particular <m>x</m> value.</p>
<p>Take a look at the graph above to get a feel for the natural logarithm function. You can also review the basic definition of the <term>natural logarithm function</term> <ref to="https://mathinsight.org/logarithm_basics">here</ref>.</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

  
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Let <m>f(x) = \ln x</m>. 
Then <m>\frac{df}{dx} = </m> 
  <answer name="vid1Review2">1/x</answer>
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />  
<p>The derivative of <m>f</m>, <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>, evaluated at <m>x = 3</m> is:</p>
  <m>\frac{df}{dx} \bigg |_{x=3} = </m>
  <answer name="vid1Review3">1/3</answer>
</exercise>

</stack>

<stack>
  <number name="x" hide="true" displayDecimals="2">5</number>
<figure suppressFigureNameInCaption="true">
  <graph xmin="-2" ymin="-7">
    <xLabel><m>x</m></xLabel>
    <yLabel><m>f, g</m></yLabel>
    <function name="f" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="center">ln(x)
      <label><m>f(x) = \ln x</m></label>
    </function>
    <function name="f_prime" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="center" hide="!$showDerivative" domain="(0,infinity)" styleNumber="3">1/x
      <label><m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m></label>
    </function>
    <function name="expFunction" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="center" styleNumber="4" hide="!$showExponentialFunc">e^x</function>  
    <line styleNumber="6" fixed="true">y = x</line>
    <point styleNumber="2" name="dragPoint" displayDecimals="2" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="upperright" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">($x,$$f($x))
      <label><m>(x, \ln x) = <copy displayDecimals="2" source="$dragPoint.coords" /></m></label>
    </point>

    <point styleNumber="4" name="dragPoint2" displayDecimals="2" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="upperright" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">($x,$$expFunction($x))
      <label><m>(x, e^ x) = <coords displayDecimals="2" extend="$dragPoint2.coords" /></m></label>
    </point>

    <point styleNumber="3" name="dragPointDerivative" displayDecimals="2" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="upperright" showCoordsWhenDragging="false" hide="!$showDerivative">($x,$$f_prime($x))
      <label><m>(x, \frac{1}{x})</m></label>
    </point>
   
    <point styleNumber="5" name="fixedPoint" displayDecimals="2" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="upperright" fixed="true">(e^(1/2),$$f(e^(1/2)))
      <label><m>\bigg($fixedPoint.x, \frac{1}{2}\bigg)</m></label>
    </point>
  
    <legend>
      <label><m>f(x) = \ln x</m></label>
      <label><m>f'(x) = 1/x</m></label>
      <label><m>g(x) = e^x</m></label>
    </legend>
  </graph>
<caption>Graph of <m>f(x) = \ln x</m> and <m>g(x) = e^x</m></caption>
</figure>
<booleanInput name="showDerivative">
    <label>Show <m>f'(x)</m></label>
</booleanInput>
<booleanInput name="showExponentialFunc" prefill="true">
    <label>Show <m>g(x)</m></label>
</booleanInput>
</stack>
</sideBySide>

<aside><title>Formula - The derivative of the <term>natural logarithm function</term>:</title>
  <md>
    <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \frac{1}{x}\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;f(x) = \ln x</mrow>
  </md>

  <md>
    <mrow>\textbf{Differential form:}\;\;\; \frac{d}{dx} \bigg(\ln x \bigg)\amp= \frac{1}{x}\;\;\;</mrow>
  </md>

  
  <number name="x" hide="true" displayDecimals="2">5</number>
  <graph xmin="-2" ymin="-7">
    <xLabel><m>x</m></xLabel>
    <function name="f" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="center">ln(x)
      <label><m>f(x) = \ln x</m></label>
    </function>
    <derivative name="f_prime" styleNumber="3" domain="(0,infinity)" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="left">$f
      <label><m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m></label>
    </derivative>
    <line styleNumber="2" slope="$$f_prime($x)" through="$dragPoint" name="derivLine" />

    <point styleNumber="2" name="dragPoint" displayDecimals="2" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="upperright" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">($x,$$f($x))
      <label><m>f'($x) = <math displayDecimals="2" simplify="true">$$f_prime($x)</math></m></label>
    </point>
    <point styleNumber="5" name="dragPointDerivative" displayDecimals="2" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="left" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">($x,$$f_prime($x))
      <label><m>($x, <math displayDecimals="2">$$f_prime($x)</math>)</m></label>
    </point>
    <legend>
      <label><m>f(x) = \ln x</m></label>
      <label><m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m></label>
    </legend>
  </graph>
</aside>

</problem>

  
</section>